{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for retained-files draft2 at e8e0b436b63c0a5d0fa6aae0a87ed3ae21f0c684. Two remaining required findings.\n\nF1 [P1] Fence every upload completion and cleanup against the row it still owns (notes/2026-10-21-retained-files.md:295, also :260 and :285).\nThe four-step upload does not retain an attempt identity or recheck ownership before failed verification removes the row and orders deletion. A concrete permitted sequence is: PUT A reserves digestD; while A reads its body, an act carries the correct small file beside its intent and commits D as named, which section5 explicitly permits; A then fails its digest check. Step3 removes the now-named row and orders deletion of the valid bytes committed by that act. If the act also redacted D before A returns, the same stale failure can erase the redacted row and its permanent refusal. Two PUTs can also both proceed from pending, allowing an older failure to erase a newer success. Serialized row transactions do not serialize those outstanding bodies and callbacks.\n\nDefine ownership across the whole upload, including in-flight retries, with a transfer identity/generation or an explicit single-owner rule. Every success, failure, timeout and cleanup must recheck that identity and current row state after asynchronous work. A stale transfer must never delete canonical bytes or remove/replace a held, named, redacted or newer pending row. State how temporary bytes are isolated or how all writers and deletion are fenced. Add the late-failed-upload-after-inline-capture witness and overlapping-PUT success/failure witness, including redaction before the old callback. If sweep safety depends on an upload deadline or lease, name and enforce that bound; a statement about the Worker's request time alone does not define one. This is the remaining part of draft1F1/F2, not a new feature request.\n\nF2 [P2] Give the SQLite byte table the same incarnation identity as the bucket (notes/2026-10-21-retained-files.md:206-207).\nThe narrative, file rows and bucket key now carry the incarnation, but retained_file still has only PRIMARY KEY(digest), with no inc column. Main's Worker addresses its scope object by ScopeId (worker.ts:168, :337), while incarnation is minted separately. This table cannot represent two independent copies of the same digest for the same scope ID's two incarnations. Redacting the new incarnation's copy deletes the one physical row needed by the old incarnation, contrary to section4 and witness13. Make the SQLite key and every get/head/put/delete include incarnation, for example PRIMARY KEY(inc,digest), or specify an equally explicit separate-store namespace per full scope reference. File-row filtering alone does not isolate the byte table. Run witness13 against the SQLite path as RF2 already requires. This is the remaining part of draft1F3.\n\nDraft1F4 is closed: RF2 now owns the small-file upload route, pending/sweep behavior and their reachable witnesses, while RF3 owns the bucket timing measurement. The ordinary tombstone-first delete-failure/restart path, post-capture repeated PUT and bucket incarnation addressing are substantial corrections. Their guarantees still need the asynchronous ownership fence above. The rest of the retained-or-published, field/domain, read, replay, export and slice design was reread in full and has no additional required finding in this review.\n\nI read all553primary lines first. Primary88edeccf is effective, fresh, live and not describing a superseded world, under live planner promiseacc6b225 for request070940da. Native prepare assigns this exact source. Cited Main603220b9 evidence was read from physically distinct copies of explicit, hash-verified leaves of the frozen draft1 review; no completed helper or root was executed or edited. This candidate adds only the30,002B note, SHA256ab13c488695d23dd14ea15c145e887c56faf9a396531daaabd5277ddb7b44a02. All2,621 Main paths and modes are unchanged. Whitespace check passe